quot;&quot;&gt; introduces TRAIL (Threat and Risk Analysis Informed Lifecycle), a threat modeling process developed by Trail of Bits that combines elements from existing methodologies like Mozilla&amp;#39;s Rapid Risk Assessment (RRA) and NIST guidelines. TRAIL analyzes connections between system components to uncover design-level weaknesses and architectural risks, going beyond individual vulnerabilities. The process involves building a detailed system model, identifying threat actor paths, and documenting threat scenarios, as well as including short-term mitigation options and long-term strategic recommendations. The post gives examples from ToB[0xe2][0x80][0x99]s assessments of Arch Linux Pacman and Linkerd.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; align=&quot;left&quot; style=&quot;padding:0px 15px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; align=&quot;left&quot; style=&quot;padding:0px 15px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;a class=&quot;link&quot; href=&quot;https://link.mail.beehiiv.com/ss/c/u001.BnxzMnTWmB9lwbaT23otsdgVNjWIU8uupc9PO5-N4y69mPglqyqUVoF1qpvHEcvmmE3vVh5WbuvcFQxCKZYKqqHlLFffROXcf64-z8lS6Aj9xi1qUtwlyIapt7gk9ippRVOinV8xncjnEVXQDA9b-eyYOoP1ZOoPZkcMUUsVPOrzGd_3ClEAEyDK50cS_cHNWbG4PZ2JhZ8FJ_jH_MJJXXogxtrM6HvhdvO-cXl1bctLe72vIuFo_DHgTLjEXA1KGEzTniV21D3aC05-R7Gf0c9m99qWs8g8VMMYTTX-xctxfeHzBDMh6DW1CX9_fpKu3gUbwSQt3pPrOYhq9F5qsrIVlhYMeY6CYD56dW3y6cA/4ey/YDCb4n53TZe70uu_eMp2Gw/h10/h001.pt8aBjmWP_Z1JIE4y-AizS0jrccXsByOoJNk_RgAnRU&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer nofollow&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;Continuous TRAIL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Follow-up post to the above: Spencer Michaels, Pawe[0xc5][0x82] P[0xc5][0x82]atek, and &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;a class=&quot;link&quot; href=&quot;https://link.mail.beehiiv.com/ss/c/u001.-QQnKmN9995ElcpR565Sh-SDvgyu6m58HhCZ9db8DGJxS6p1zUgMA2q8x2lps6OHKp3MSH_DYp6h22cUiblANj27zf52-cnPcpKj7cpUnQVuScGjez699OnknAfd7ztTHbV42Df2PWAl0sow3cVAmhrkNl1nnyucqZozuFzEmfCNGXh5_fHsp3dP_JdnFJ4JIrSkOBo81ziY7YAqR052DZFoy61ScR0d2OSNb2WfeCfS7QW4RQJYa2-Od0BxcKusBEjxP2x4pP8b3OQ6KjhA5haG-X9ENXyVa15NAhz-1-NRcg2Ez0ha9RuL1ZhW7-tDlKsEv13pv_3zXCz1d49tyw/4ey/YDCb4n53TZe70uu_eMp2Gw/h11/h001.hOMjgKRh9q-kQ3FKn1k-BPkf21f_JcTStclG9Sq9t5c&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer nofollow&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;Kelly Kaoudis&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt; describe how to further tailor a TRAIL threat model, how to maintain it, when to update it as development continues, and how to make use of it. Focus on keeping up to date: the trust zones, threat actors, trust zone connections, and security-relevant assumptions. Questions to consider when deciding when to update your threat model:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td style=&quot;padding-bottom:12px;padding-left:37px;padding-right:27px;padding-top:12px;&quot; class=&quot;ee&quot;&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;margin-left:0px;&quot; class=&quot;edm_outlooklist&quot;&gt;&lt;ul style=&quot;list-style-type:disc;margin:0px 0px;padding:0px 0px 0px 0px;&quot;&gt;&lt;li class=&quot;listItem ultext&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;padding:0px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Does this change add a new system component (e.g., microservice, module, major feature, or third-party integration)?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li class=&quot;listItem ultext&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;padding:0px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Does this change add a new trust zone (e.g., by adding a new network segment)?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li class=&quot;listItem ultext&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;padding:0px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Does this change introduce a new threat actor (e.g., a new user role)?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li class=&quot;listItem ultext&q"
[2025-04-15 21:52:22,359][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-20 << "uot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;padding:0px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Does this change add a new connection between system components that crosses a boundary between trust zones (e.g., a new application service on an existing server instance that can be called by a service in a different zone)?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; align=&quot;left&quot; style=&quot;padding:0px 15px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;&quot;&gt; [0xf0][0x9f][0x92][0xa1]&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt; Expert human-driven threat models are obviously the best, but I think there[0xe2][0x80][0x99]s a lot of promise in leveraging LLMs (and program analysis) to do continuous threat model updates by analyzing design docs/new feature specs and code changes. There are a number of start-ups and OSS tools doing this, exciting times.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; align=&quot;left&quot; style=&quot;padding:0px 15px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;&lt;table role=&quot;none&quot; width=&quot;100%&quot; border=&quot;0&quot; cellspacing=&quot;0&quot; cellpadding=&quot;0&quot; style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td bgcolor=&quot;#d9edd9&quot; style=&quot;background-color:#d9edd9;padding:5.0px 5.0px 5.0px 5.0px;&quot;&gt;&lt;table role=&quot;none&quot; width=&quot;100%&quot; border=&quot;0&quot; cellspacing=&quot;0&quot; cellpadding=&quot;0&quot;&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; align=&quot;center&quot; style=&quot;padding:0px 15px;text-align:center;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Sponsor&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; align=&quot;center&quot; valign=&quot;top&quot; style=&quot;color:#2A2A2A;font-weight:normal;padding:0px 15px;text-align:center;&quot;&gt;&lt;h1 style=&quot;color:#2A2A2A;font-weight:normal;&quot;&gt;[0xf0][0x9f][0x93][0xa3]&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;[0xc2][0xa0]&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;a class=&quot;link&quot; href=&quot;https://link.mail.beehiiv.com/ss/c/u001.24Nk1afHpCiQnIZ62Q0ozj7UmKhz1HEla2gYkyAkFF35MPWt2q_bR_5N5B7WYWr2Zc92cbPmGVYUVfUU2ruuHT7p8afGHtpMQFmjUEKlOmhfIMtrg2KjGfY-iZrLlwG9_9j4JwLjKsjZ4_o3U617DyQqrHzTVdbMfEhWnhWH3vQokZkgHLnkE3GPYgqJJxgfDQu1Tc9fHn0ZP0O0aDH7bMrd-j3ItEap7spwR6_QsRT66LBDnoqn4-3g7PR7j_9k/4ey/YDCb4n53TZe70uu_eMp2Gw/h12/h001.VpMnXhSOiA0hmN1yRg6-QcW-f2xKKzb8JYA76L97Oyw&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer nofollow&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;The Security Canary Maturity Model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td align=&quot;center&quot; valign=&quot;top&quot; style=&quot;font-size:0px;line-height:0px;padding:5px 0px;&quot; class=&quot;dd&quot;&gt;&lt;table class=&quot;j&quot; role=&quot;none&quot; width=&quot;96%&quot; border=&quot;0&quot; cellspacing=&quot;0&quot; cellpadding=&quot;0&quot; align=&quot;center&quot;&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt; &amp;nbsp; &lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; align=&quot;left&quot; style=&quot;padding:0px 15px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;No matter the maturity level of your overall program, there is room for canaries. In this work, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;a class=&quot;link&quot; href=&quot;https://link.mail.beehiiv.com/ss/c/u001.-QQnKmN9995ElcpR565Sh-