;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt; (previously in &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;tl;dr sec&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;). Ian shows how an attacker could create a malicious VM image with a matching offer name (e.g. &amp;quot;GitHub-Enterprise&amp;quot;) that appears in search results, potentially tricking users into deploying it. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; align=&quot;left&quot; style=&quot;padding:0px 15px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; align=&quot;left&quot; style=&quot;padding:0px 15px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;a class=&quot;link&quot; href=&quot;https://link.mail.beehiiv.com/ss/c/u001.BnxzMnTWmB9lwbaT23otsVtXpRlnv12AI9oOg8QJVBUpEvYvzkfqUwV4fcn1m7WyhhgBkZ4yTmBEFqri-C5mt8gnHT-Qeh-_3SE4YR0mPceUCGdvgbmBUSPZp5Bj68hQTEsuIcS1r4kxN1zD6T-Qi-YOBeZ1_-I_F7yNfwgjM6zKL1ggtLJwMu6WuxyPPkzMYXrX56cggavhI2PzKzuRC7g6h9Aql2i0ZWpZEyRBHBSs9Su7jVBnjcPGJmN6T33J5mbnYiHWoc2pihsjuieykYHjYSidIcyj7zW6pvce3n_VGg_FxVuBL84HJS9a4uXKo7Sb2CVdP_fpGOcgLYmPkZyxNR8FaYUeOK1kuPl3_2s/4ey/YDCb4n53TZe70uu_eMp2Gw/h18/h001.1vvcEghmVnrYalDoOCsFDGFzcc_YBv4iR9psS5sXX3M&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer nofollow&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;The Risk You Can&amp;#39;t Afford to Ignore: AWS SES and Email Spoofing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;a class=&quot;link&quot; href=&quot;https://link.mail.beehiiv.com/ss/c/u001.-QQnKmN9995ElcpR565Sh-SDvgyu6m58HhCZ9db8DGJ9QXE0mNiYf8GrR8ZrTu958E79SX_ZBB9DmA_-FsxiANjABMr3gdAT_b_5q6gktM8f2uxjgjnoQGAmqrAlWjPWj3D6blzqDpnL-Xf6I3ln2HOoSxGeUKoWqCloFrKva6gclmsgaLHL-J8CoeNkFnqXj9OX1MockJtTvLZni8vBubAgenOoUrsU4wnhVLE0TlD6A8VPEJ-gBZe5bc5R_FHw8IAulNGCVDTIwy9uTGop3SFnXhwGIgeG40NvNJq_hAc9mFKv85gUdot3De6m1HuhOZCKv2vs0qXJGgM4iCZT1A/4ey/YDCb4n53TZe70uu_eMp2Gw/h19/h001.mpoKTQM5PPpMEc0TJLiR-67K_lkOZ3wtReiCGUA5JWk&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer nofollow&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;Chandrapal Badshah&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt; describes a hidden risk in AWS Simple Email Service (SES) where verifying an entire domain allows any IAM user with the right permissions to spoof any email address in that domain. By default, these emails aren&amp;#39;t logged, making detection difficult. He recommends using individually verified email identities where possible, or applying a Sending Authorization policy to allowlist specific email addresses.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; align=&quot;left&quot; style=&quot;padding:0px 15px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; align=&quot;left&quot; style=&quot;padding:0px 15px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;a class=&quot;link&quot; href=&quot;https://link.mail.beehiiv.com/ss/c/u001.SKfDuyn6ZS5YfcIWqGkNjfjyqBgThAGord5wZqW11knCetn2-4AMre_qH3IMzEEu2MAPl9gZhwbuqQSnw5qeZZtUM5IXAtIUhMnInjwsmwk43azNEmBKejPRfY-NfYxiUGo_nCYRWJNLLdhJ0wbl7jhM23LXcNCTaqzAcnVKnMd7KloDaFMSX-8jD_u97APBSiwP8NF23fv2Z1EK_HVB_29aP_gR05ENiGNB23ACwnmE0nyLlaW9evaiB5JNfTyL7UDK-aHnhHqMkKy1kTnDeM-tXZtyHAyhAxz6Ync-4mroV0vbOnGnLyC0owbNj-h0rXXLrVvQ5CCtyO1rrawSt5qTzKtkl1UmlhZQYDW8HiuJAktO6CfgxR_-lPizx_RjavNt_-HB7p3_o2h5jrh-qEGsY4qQSe8Ewv3-zpj2ADYQXYyPc9kdnej7gtfDQOfr/4ey/YDCb4n53TZe70uu_eMp2Gw/h20/h001.-KeKM27ZIK1jVGVRhH-bs2ipn8i4bFNfMbxIqpQdtbs&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer nofollow&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;Evaluating AWS Native Approaches for Detecting Suspicious API Calls&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;a class=&quot;link&quot; href=&quot;https://link.mail.beehiiv.com/ss/c/u001.-QQnKmN9995ElcpR565Sh-SDvgyu6m58HhCZ9db8DGLhXouycvYdN6G0Rlj97adUE_HJxoEGgtjNEV4K6p9-PAX-6uWxhAjI7ZEVbZCcqAGuAuv-ZxqvCrmsYPoTCRi2jV99PQ7K7GfbX7x0CL7QYgsC3X63BeozjEoDvmB2F3YGEKJVATb_zybgRKrKzktGNBKAmOlnWeBaRTMwDePbFCu_EndsW5M4Nq7Ubh8QKgzQhsf9cktThDPxzly62GaGbOEgr33Xafst-82ZP30URiAN3bXKYjPRTqaT5vDghoxqZaeDVij_Z5sfGoGmya8in0qIWc5drpA5JOPwezsyZjmd-z1CvcmvKNegonKLlNukeKckrs2Tk421quNNX-X-/4ey/YDCb4n53TZe70uu_eMp2Gw/h21/h001.XLsbWofEBS-UKdcuT_xiCd236K2WDYHF0HIocZsxC80&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer nofollow&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;Adan Alvarez&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt; describes evaluates three AWS native approaches for detecting suspicious API calls: 1) EventBridge [0xe2][0x86][0x92] SNS [0xe2][0x86][0x92] Email, 2) CloudTrail [0xe2][0x86][0x92] S3 [0xe2][0x86][0x92] Lambda [0xe2][0x86][0x92] SNS [0xe2][0x86][0x92] Email, and 3) CloudTrail [0xe2][0x86][0x92] CloudWatch [0xe2][0x86][0x92] MetricFilter [0xe2][0x86][0x92] MetricAlert [0xe2][0x86][0x92] SNS [0xe2][0x86][0x92] Email. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; align=&quot;left&quot; style=&quot;padding:0px 15px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Choose 1) if you clearly know what to do when a specific event occurs. 3) is great for simple alerts. If manual investigation is expected and speed is less important choose 2) or 3), and 2) is the best choice if you require more complex alerts with additional context.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; align=&quot;left&quot; style=&quot;padding:0px 15px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; al